course: world
lesson: 96
title: Laws, voting and how a country is run
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A country has millions of people, so they cannot all meet in one room to decide things. In a democracy the people choose a smaller group to decide for them, by voting in an election.
explain: The people they choose are called representatives. They meet in a parliament — in the United States it is called Congress — where they argue about ideas and vote on new laws. A plan for a new law is called a bill; it becomes a law only after enough of them vote for it.
explain: Power is split into three parts so that no one person can do whatever they like: lawmakers make the laws, the government carries them out, and the courts decide whether a law has been broken. Each part can check the others, like three players who all watch that the game is fair.
explain: The most basic rules of how a country is run are written in its constitution. Even the leader must obey the constitution and the laws.
explain: Voting is secret: you mark your choice alone, so nobody can punish or reward you for it. That is why people can vote honestly, even against powerful people.
fact: In a democracy the people choose their leaders and lawmakers by voting.
fact: In most elections the person or party with the most votes wins.
fact: In a secret ballot nobody can see how a person voted.
fact: In the United States, the United Kingdom, Canada and Australia, citizens can vote in national elections from the age of 18.
fact: A parliament is a group of elected representatives who debate and make laws; in the United States it is called Congress.
fact: A plan for a new law is called a bill.
fact: A constitution is the set of basic rules for how a country is run; the United States Constitution was written in 1787.
fact: Power is shared between lawmakers, the government and the courts, so that each can check the others.
fact: Courts and judges decide whether a law has been broken and must listen fairly to both sides.
fact: The President of the United States is elected every four years and can serve at most two terms, that is, two four-year turns.
fact: The United Kingdom has a king, but the country is run by a government led by the Prime Minister.
fact: In Australia every adult citizen must vote, or pay a small fine.
exercise: How do people in a democracy choose their leaders? | By voting in elections | By the oldest family ruling | By the strongest army deciding | By the king choosing alone
exercise: What is a parliament? | A group of elected people who make laws | A court where the judges punish all crimes | The house where the king lives | An office that collects all the taxes
exercise: What is the United States parliament called? | Congress | The Cabinet | The Supreme Court | The White House
exercise: What is a bill? | A plan for a new law that is voted on | A law that can never be changed | A list of all the people allowed to vote | A judge's decision about a crime
exercise: What is a constitution? | The basic rules for how a country is run | A list of all the country's cities | The long speech a leader gives each new year | A map showing a country's borders
exercise: Why is voting secret? | So nobody can punish or reward people for their vote | So that the counting goes much faster | So the winner stays a surprise for years | So people do not have to choose at all
exercise: Why is power split between lawmakers, the government and the courts? | So that no one person or group has all the power | So that every job needs three times more people | So that laws can be made without any voting | So that the leader can overrule all the judges
exercise: Who decides whether a law has been broken? | Courts and judges | The person who was harmed | The police officer alone | The newspapers
exercise: From what age can citizens vote in national elections in the USA, UK, Canada and Australia? | 18 | 12 | 16 | 25
exercise: How often is the President of the United States elected? | Every four years | Every year | Every ten years | Only once for life
exercise: Who runs the government of the United Kingdom? | The Prime Minister and the ministers | The king on his own | The judges of the highest court in the land | The mayor of London
exercise: In which country must every adult citizen vote? | Australia | The United States | The United Kingdom | Canada
exercise: Why does a country choose representatives instead of everyone deciding every law? | Millions of people cannot all debate every law together | Ordinary people are not allowed to have opinions | Representatives never make any mistakes | Laws need to be made by just one person
exercise: Must a country's leader obey the laws and the constitution? | Yes, the leader must obey them like everyone else | No, the leader is above all the laws | Only the laws that the leader made personally | Only while an election is taking place
test: Your class votes for a class captain: Mia gets 12 votes, Sam 9 and Leo 7. Who wins under the usual rule? | Mia, because she has the most votes | Leo, because he has the fewest votes | Nobody, because no one got every vote | Sam, because he is in the middle
test: A powerful man stands next to the voting booth and watches what each person marks. Which rule is being broken? | The secret ballot | The age to vote | The constitution's date | The number of parties
test: Lawmakers vote in favour of a bill. What has the bill become? | A law | A constitution | A court case | An election
test: Which is true about a democracy? | People can vote out leaders they do not like | The leader's child always rules next | Only soldiers are allowed to vote | Elections happen only when the leader wants them to
test: A leader wants to ignore a court's decision and change the rules alone. Why would the three-part split of power stop this? | The courts and lawmakers can check the leader | The leader has much more power than everyone else | Judges always agree with the leader | The constitution lets leaders do anything
test: A 15-year-old in Canada wants to vote for the national parliament. What happens? | She is too young; the voting age is 18 | She can vote if her parents agree | She can vote but only in secret | She is old enough to vote in every election
test: In a trial, the judge listens only to one side and refuses to hear the other. What is wrong? | Judges must listen fairly to both sides | Judges must always side with the police | Trials must be decided by the president | Nothing; judges may choose whom to hear
test: Which is a true difference between the USA and the UK? | The USA elects a president; the UK has a king and a prime minister | The UK elects a president; the USA has a king | Both are ruled by a king with no elections | Neither country has any parliament at all
test: An Australian adult decides not to vote in the election. What can happen? | They may have to pay a small fine | Nothing, voting there is only a choice | They lose their citizenship for life | Their vote is given to the winner
test: how come no one can see who i voted for | It is a secret ballot, so nobody can see how a person voted | Votes are thrown away after counting | Only the winner can see the votes | The teacher reads them all at the end
test: whats a bill in parliament, like a shop bill? | No, it is a plan for a new law | Yes, it is what the government owes | Yes, it is a list of things to buy | No, it is a court's final decision
test: Leo says in the UK the king runs the country. true? | No, the country is run by a government led by the Prime Minister | Yes, the king makes all the laws | Yes, but only in the summer | No, it is run by the courts
test: my cousin reckons the US president can be elected as many times as he likes. right? | No, the president can serve at most two terms | Yes, there is no limit at all | Yes, as long as he wins each time | No, the president can only ever serve one term of four years
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a democracy? | The people choose their leaders and lawmakers by voting | A single ruler makes every law without any vote | The judges choose the leaders of the country | The leaders are chosen by drawing lots
exam: Identify the designation of a plan for a new law. | A bill | A ballot | A constitution | A verdict
exam: Ascertain the maximum number of terms which a President of the United States may serve. | Two terms of four years | One term of eight years | Three terms of four years | An unlimited number of terms
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the sharing of power between lawmakers, the government and the courts. | Each can check the others | The courts make all the laws alone | The government is free of every check | The lawmakers judge every case in court
